<!--
  LOGIN LINK — how someone gets into the support-only account their submission created.

  A SEPARATE EMAIL, on the client's instruction: "We can keep the emails separate." It is also the
  right split on its own terms. The acknowledgement is a receipt and it must arrive whether or not
  the address is ever verified; this one is a credential, it is requested rather than triggered, and
  it expires. Folding a credential into a receipt means every receipt carries a live key.

  ── VARIABLES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    {{ login_url }}       The single-use link. Never shortened, never wrapped by a tracker — see the
                          scanner note below.
    {{ link_expires }}    A human duration for the LINK's lifetime, e.g. `15 minutes`. This is the
                          only number in this directory that is allowed to name a length of time,
                          because it describes a key we control and not an answer we have not
                          written. Everything about the wait is still unquantified.
    {{ request_url }}     Where to ask for another one.
    {{ support_address }} The fallback inbox.

  ── THIS IS THE ONE WITH A BUTTON, AND THEREFORE THE ONE WITH THE ACCENT ────────────────────────
  Across the three templates Signal Teal marks what can be acted on and nothing that is merely being
  read. Here that is a single solid teal button, which is the only solid fill in the whole set: this
  email has exactly one job and the reader should be able to find it without reading a word. The
  acknowledgement gets teal on two text links; the reply gets it on one. That ordering is the design,
  not a coincidence of content.

  ── WHAT IT IS FOR, SAID BEFORE THE LINK ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Someone who filed a ticket did not set out to make an account, so the first sentence has to explain
  a thing they did not ask for. It names what the account holds — the cases opened from their address
  — and nothing else, because that is all it holds. It is not a Baseout product account and must
  never be described as one.

  ── LINK SCANNERS ARE THE REAL FAILURE MODE, AND IT IS IN THE COPY ──────────────────────────────
  Corporate mail filters — Microsoft Defender Safe Links and its peers — follow links
  programmatically before delivery, which consumes a single-use one. Baseout's buyer is a technical
  ops person, i.e. disproportionately behind exactly such a gateway, so this is not an edge case for
  this audience. There is no template-side fix; the fix is a six-digit code, which is a different
  build. What the copy can do is make the failure legible instead of baffling, and offer the way out
  in the same breath. That is the third paragraph, and it is load bearing.

  ── THE PRINTED URL GETS ITS OWN LABELLED LINE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  It used to trail a sentence. A URL is a thing to copy, not a thing to read, and burying one in
  prose asks the reader to find where it starts. It now sits under the label rung on its own line,
  which is the same treatment the receipt gives a value, so a person on a locked-down desktop can
  select it in one drag.

  ── NO CLOCK ON THE ANSWER ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  The link's own lifetime is stated. Nothing here says anything about how long a reply takes.

  ── AUTO-GENERATED, AND A REPLY MUST NOT OPEN A CASE ────────────────────────────────────────────
  This is machine-sent, so it carries the auto-generated header. Its reply-to should be the support
  address rather than a case route: somebody who answers a sign-in link with "I didn't ask for this"
  is reporting a security concern, not adding to a ticket.
-->
<mjml>
  <mj-head>
    <mj-include path="./partials/head.mjml" />
    <mj-preview>Use this link to sign in to the Baseout support portal.</mj-preview>
  </mj-head>

  <mj-body>
    <mj-section padding="0"><mj-column><mj-spacer height="32px" /></mj-column></mj-section>

    <mj-wrapper>
      <!-- NO BAND. The logo stands on the card with air around it, which is the classic letterhead.
           The black masthead is gone from all three real emails and survives only in style two, which
           is now the variable that pair tests. Removing it here as well was not asked for and is not
           taste: three transactional messages from one sender that do not look like one sender is a
           defect, and consistency across the set outranks the reason any single one changed.

           THE COST IS THE LOGO SWAP. With no pinned band the logo sits on the card, and the card is
           white in one theme and #111111 in the other, so there are two files again and a conditional
           to keep Word from showing both. The argument is in `partials/head.mjml`. -->
      <mj-section border-radius="12px 12px 0 0" css-class="bo-band-top" padding="52px 32px 0">
        <mj-column>
          <mj-raw>
            <img src="https://support.baseout.com/email/baseout-logo-ink@2x.png" alt="Baseout" width="150" height="40" class="bo-logo-light" style="display: block; width: 150px; height: 40px; border: 0; outline: none; text-decoration: none; color: #156e97; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -0.01em;" />
            <!--[if !mso]><!-->
            <img src="https://support.baseout.com/email/baseout-logo-cyan@2x.png" alt="Baseout" width="150" height="40" class="bo-logo-dark" style="display: none; width: 150px; height: 40px; border: 0; outline: none; text-decoration: none; color: #5cceff; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -0.01em;" />
            <!--<![endif]-->
          </mj-raw>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>

      <mj-section padding="28px 32px 0">
        <mj-column>
          <mj-text mj-class="eyebrow" css-class="bo-eyebrow">SUPPORT ACCOUNT</mj-text>
          <mj-spacer height="10px" />
          <mj-text mj-class="h1">Your sign-in link</mj-text>
          <mj-spacer height="12px" />
          <mj-text>
            This link signs you in to the Baseout support portal, where you can read the cases opened
            from your email address. It is a support account only, not a Baseout account, and it
            gives no access to your data.
          </mj-text>

          <mj-spacer height="24px" />
          <mj-button href="{{ login_url }}" align="left">Sign in to support</mj-button>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>

      <!-- THE URL IS PRINTED AS WELL AS LINKED, in the same inset block the other two use for a value
           the reader handles rather than reads. A button is an image-shaped thing to a client that
           strips styling, and a person on a locked-down desktop often has to copy the address into a
           browser by hand. It is NEUTRAL rather than the acknowledgement's tinted callout on purpose:
           this is the fallback for the button above it, and a tinted block here would argue with the
           one control the email exists to offer. -->
      <mj-section padding="28px 32px 0">
        <mj-column background-color="#f6f7fb" border="1px solid #d5d7de" border-radius="8px" padding="16px 20px" css-class="bo-quotebox">
          <mj-text mj-class="label" css-class="bo-muted">OR PASTE THIS INTO YOUR BROWSER</mj-text>
          <mj-spacer height="6px" />
          <mj-text mj-class="muted" css-class="bo-muted bo-break">{{ login_url }}</mj-text>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>

      <mj-section padding="32px 32px 40px">
        <mj-column>
          <mj-text mj-class="muted" css-class="bo-muted">
            The link expires {{ link_expires }} after this email was sent, and it works once. If it
            has expired, or if it reports that it has already been used, ask for a new one at
            <a href="{{ request_url }}" class="bo-break" style="color: #156e97;">{{ request_url }}</a>. Some corporate mail
            filters open links before you do, which can use a link up before it reaches you; asking
            for another is the fix, and you can ask as often as you need.
          </mj-text>

          <mj-spacer height="12px" />
          <mj-text mj-class="muted" css-class="bo-muted">
            If you did not ask for this, ignore it. Nothing changes until the link is used, and it
            only works from this email. If you keep receiving these, tell us at
            <a href="mailto:{{ support_address }}" class="bo-break" style="color: #156e97;">{{ support_address }}</a>.
          </mj-text>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>

    </mj-wrapper>

    <mj-section padding="0"><mj-column><mj-spacer height="32px" /></mj-column></mj-section>
  </mj-body>
</mjml>
